[Applause]  freedom day in england july 19  the day the country lifted almost all  coronavirus restrictions  other parts of the uk soon followed  but did it come too early  freedom day meant no more masks and no  more social distancing  about half the adult population was  fully vaccinated officials were  confident the threat to public health  had been minimized  but now britain has among the highest  confirmed cases in europe  more than 40 000 a day  welcome i'm ben fazoulen medical experts  are calling on the british government to  bring back social distancing the  government is refusing relying instead  on booster shots to protect the most  vulnerable  this is how the uk wants to get through  the winter a third covet 19 shot for 30  million vulnerable people  but many vaccination centers have closed  and the vaccination program is sluggish  76 year old iva garfinkel died while  waiting for his booster shot his son  blames the slow pace  he was literally  going out shopping going into his  hospital checkups and things like that  and he caught covered  and um 11 days later um he died i  genuinely do think a booster might have  saved his life  case numbers are the highest in europe  but a vaccination rate above 67  has kept down severe infections and  deaths  after lifting all restrictions in  england the government strategy seemed  to be working but the tide is turning  the uk registered 223 deaths on tuesday  nearly a thousand people were  hospitalized  we have relatively low  social distancing measures  compared to much of europe the other big  thing is  um i think  immunizations that immunity from either  previous infections or immunizations are  almost certainly beginning to wane  the projection offered by the  astrazeneca vaccine the most widely used  in the uk appears to decline  significantly over time  but boosters are urgently needed for  other vaccines too  the uk's national health service says  it's time to act they're calling for the  government to trigger its plan b a set  of measures including mandatory mask  wearing vaccination passes in certain  settings and recommending people work  from home  the government is rejecting calls to  trigger fresh restrictions  at a news conference on wednesday the  health minister said he expects up to a  hundred thousand cases a day in the  coming weeks which means more severe  infections and deaths are likely  inevitable  he appealed to the public for voluntary  support  if we want to secure these freedoms for  the long term  then the best thing that we can do is  come forward once again when that moment  comes there are many more things that we  can all do  to help contain the spread of this virus  like  you know meeting outdoors where it's  possible and if you can only meet  indoors letting in fresh air like  wearing a face covering in crowded  enclosed spaces  when asked why ministers in a packed  parliament weren't wearing masks the  health minister conceded good point and  said lawmakers should set an example  winter is coming and healthcare workers  are already feeling the pressure  yet another concerning new variant is  making its rounds in the coming weeks  may well be decisive for the united  kingdom  let's bring in heidi larson director of  the vaccine confidence project at the  london school of hygiene and tropical  medicine she joins us from brussels  did freedom day come too early for  england in your opinion  yes i think it was a bit um freedom day  was perhaps a bit premature but  i think that you know we have to think  about freedom in relative terms because  um  i  i think too much too soon is is  um  let's put it this way the  pandemic is highly volatile i don't  think anywhere in the world  is ready for full freedoms if you think  of  it kind of frames restrictions as being  not freedom but we're actually trying to  get freedom from the disease  so yes uh in trying to throw off all the  restrictions in one go  it was probably too early i think it  needs to be a  an evolving process  i mean it sounds like a great public  relations campaign but when you look at  the reality of things uh the vaccination  rate for example isn't it far too low  for such freedoms  yes and it's not just about i mean  vaccinations definitely have lulled in  terms of uptake there was a real peak  during the summer and all the willing  were coming forward now we're in a more  difficult stage but i think it's it's a  harsh reminder  that it's not just about the vaccines i  mean people have also shed masks  distancing um are more lacks about that  the gatherings um are you know there are  gatherings of a lot of people uh all the  things that  viruses love  um for for propagating are are now  you know there for them uh there for the  virus so i think that um  we we need to  take this in cautious steps and and  there will be some freedoms in some ways  but um i i think we can't do it all in  one go without having some situation  like now where it's the viruses is  coming back  so how do you get the rest of the  population vaccinated  well i think  it's hard to ask  although what i was going to say people  are going to need patients but it's a  hard ask 18 plus months almost two years  into the pandemic  people are feeling pretty impatient but  also it's taken an economic toll on some  people it certainly affected schooling  so i under the understand the government  wanting to  please the people as it were with the  sense that we're getting closer to  normal  um  or whatever  normal was  um  but but i do think uh  this story is not over at all we can see  other places in the world that  had it under control a bit more like in  asia and now are facing resurgence um  eastern europe is is really having quite  a a challenging time  uh so this is uh  we really still need to be quite  cautious i think some freedoms some  mobility but you can have more mobility  if people comply with with masks at the  end of the day it's all about human  behavior  and  well heidi what about portugal and the  scandinavian countries will they  encounter the same problems with their  freedom days  well i think for portugal and  scandinavia and any other country  considering opening uh freedoms that  they're going to need to be  careful with how they do it i think the  lesson from the uk is not to do  everything  stop restrictions across the board  while people are still unvaccinated and  while the virus is still raging so i  think if they  do it with steps it will be fine  um  let's see how they what decisions they  make at the time  and besides the decisions governments  make what about us what what about the  way  or the the right way for us to live with  the virus what would that be  well you it's a really important point  because not all governments are making  the best best decisions so at the end of  the day we do have our own judgment and  personally i would say given the fact  that this virus is still  hyper infective and  uh and circulating and there are quite a  few still unvaccinated people even  though we've done quite well overall in  europe uh and the uk  uh lately it's it's slowed we should  still wear i would wear masks i would  particularly encourage it and and i  think it should be required in public  transport  i would avoid crowded places um and and  just  protect yourself and and protect others  we still need to be cautious if we want  real freedom uh in the truest sense with  the virus behind us  heidi larsen london school of hygiene  and tropical medicine thank you very  much  here's a good question now for derek and  for viewers who may have just recovered  from covet 19.  how soon after having coveted 19 can a  person get it again  so you had covid maybe you got really  sick and now you hope that your  naturally acquired immunity  will protect you in the future and it  will at least to some extent but  but not perfectly and likely not forever  um  going on two years into the pandemic now  the cdc in the u.s  still says that  re-infections are rare  as time goes on though a lot of experts  believe that'll probably change  as the average person's  immunity wanes  exposure has also grown much more  widespread in many communities and the  delta variant is a lot more  transmissible  than ancestral variants so  two more factors that increase your  chances  of getting it again um one recent study  published in the lancet that was based  on pretty complex models it was a little  scary  it postulated that if sars kobe 2  becomes endemic  in people who have already had covet 19  once they could on average get it again  less than one and a half years later the  good news is that if you've had the  disease and recovered you can lower your  risk of re-infection  by getting vaccinated anyway  that doing that is a good idea is backed  up for example by evidence from the  state of kentucky  in the u.s a study there  found that people who'd been infected  and didn't get vaccinated afterwards  that they were more than twice as likely  to be re-infected as someone who did get  vaccinated afterwards in that situation  early data indicates that messenger rna  vaccines in particular  provoke an even more  powerful immune response  in recipients who've already had the  disease  it's a phenomenon that the media is  calling a superimmunity  and that turbocharged immune response  seems to occur  even after just a single shot of the  vaccine  [Music]

